var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Guide","page":"Guide","text":"This guide walks through the public surface of the package on synthetic data with a known structural coefficient β, so each step can be sanity-checked against the truth. A fuller, text-only script lives at examples/demo.jl in the repository.","title":"Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#A-synthetic-design","page":"Guide","text":"We simulate y_t = β·Y_t + u_t where Y responds to an exogenous instrument ε through a known impulse response and is endogenous (it loads on the structural error u).\n\nusing SystemProjectionsIV, Random\n\nfunction make_dgp(; T, β_true, a, ρ, loading = 1.0, seed = 20240530)\n    Random.seed!(seed)\n    ε = randn(T)                       # exogenous instrument / shock\n    u = 0.3 .* randn(T)                # structural error\n    ν = 0.2 .* randn(T)                # extra Y noise\n    Yv = zeros(T)\n    for t in 1:T, j in 0:(length(a) - 1)\n        t - j ≥ 1 && (Yv[t] += loading * a[j + 1] * ε[t - j])\n    end\n    Yv .+= ρ .* u .+ ν                 # ρ ≠ 0 ⇒ endogeneity\n    yv = β_true .* Yv .+ u\n    return yv, Yv, ε\nend\n\nT, H, β_true = 4000, 4, 0.7\na = [1.0, 0.6, 0.3, 0.15]              # IRF of Y to the shock, horizons 0..H-1\ny, Y, Z = make_dgp(; T = T, β_true = β_true, a = a, ρ = 0.8)","title":"A synthetic design"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#LP-variant-(default)","page":"Guide","text":"spiv defaults to the local-projections variant (SPIVwithLP). Inputs are y (length T), Y (T × K endogenous), and Z (T × Nz instruments) — single series can be plain vectors. An intercept is included in the controls by default.\n\nres_lp = spiv(y, Y, Z; H = H)\ncoef(res_lp)        # β̂ — compare to the true β = 0.7\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7033753437185387\n\nAdditional controls enter through keywords, and the sample is adjusted automatically (Stata/R-style): leading and trailing rows containing NaN — the padding the exported lag/lags helpers produce for lags and leads — are dropped from every input (NaN inside the sample is an error). The trim is applied to all inputs jointly, so an instrument that ends a few periods before y also shortens the outcome sample (the Fieldhouse–Mertens code keeps those outcome leads; the difference is at most max(h) observations). xlags = p controls for p lags of all the variables [y Y Z] (the same information set as the VAR variant below), while X = ... passes arbitrary extra columns (intercept = false drops the automatic constant; collinear columns, e.g. a constant in X, are dropped with a warning and Nx becomes their rank — the dof corrections then differ slightly from code that counts every column; instruments spanned by the controls are an error):\n\nres_ctrl = spiv(y, Y, Z; H = H, xlags = 4)     # lags of [y Y Z] as controls\ncoef(res_ctrl)\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7027425658508323\n\nres_lagX = spiv(y, Y, Z; H = H, X = lags(Y, 4))  # your own lagged controls\ncoef(res_lagX)\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7032989655938504\n\nThe result supports the usual StatsBase accessors (re-exported by the package):\n\ncoeftable(res_lp)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n         Coef.  Std. Error       z  Pr(>|z|)  Lower 95%  Upper 95%\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nβ[1]  0.703375  0.00397237  177.07    <1e-99    0.69559   0.711161\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconfint, coeftable, the robust set, and the IRF bands all use the level passed to spiv (default 0.95). Wald intervals use normal quantiles; the Fieldhouse–Mertens MATLAB code uses Student-t with dof_residual degrees of freedom, a difference that vanishes with T_eff.\n\nsummary prints the full estimation table, R-style:\n\nsummary(res_lp)\nSystem Projections IV — local projections (LP)\n  horizons h=0:3 (H=4), K=1 endogenous, Nz=1 instruments, Nx=1 controls, T_eff=3997\n\nCoefficients (strong-identification sandwich):\n  param      estimate   std.err.         z   95% CI\n  β[1]         0.7034     0.0040    177.07   [0.6956, 0.7112]\n\nWeak-IV (g): 1192.566   critical value: 22.104   ⇒ instruments strong\nRobust (AR, 95%, df=4): β[1]∈[0.6950, 0.7115]\nIRFs: outcome (4, 1), endogenous (4, 1, 1) (point/se/lower/upper in result.irf_*)","title":"LP variant (default)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Choosing-horizons","page":"Guide","text":"H sets the horizons stacked in the estimation. An integer means h = 0, …, H-1; a strictly increasing vector of horizons ≥ 0 stacks exactly those. Fieldhouse & Mertens, for instance, stack every fourth quarter, H = 3:4:59. The effective sample loses maximum(horizons) observations at the end, and horizons returns what was used:\n\nres_sub = spiv(y, Y, Z; H = [0, 2, 3])\n(horizons(res_sub), coef(res_sub))\n([0, 2, 3], [0.6983712999410032])\n\nRow i of every IRF block corresponds to horizons(r)[i].","title":"Choosing horizons"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#VAR-variant","page":"Guide","text":"Passing SPIVwithVAR fits a VAR(p) on the stacked [Y; y; z] and builds forecast errors from its MA representation; the controls (X, intercept, xlags) are ignored with a warning because the VAR's own lags subsume them. On the same data it should give a comparable estimate:\n\nres_var = spiv(y, Y, Z, SPIVwithVAR(1); H = H)   # VAR(1); SPIVwithVAR(p) for p lags\n(lp = coef(res_lp)[1], var = coef(res_var)[1])\n(lp = 0.7033753437185387, var = 0.703494421913865)","title":"VAR variant"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Serial-correlation-and-controls","page":"Guide","text":"The strong-identification standard errors carry no HAR correction: they are valid when the controls absorb the serial correlation of the residualised instrument z⊥ (docs/technical.md §5.4). The default controls are intercept-only (xlags = 0), which suits a serially uncorrelated shock series as instrument — as in the design above. With a persistent instrument, add lagged controls via xlags or X; xlags = p conditions on the same information as SPIVwithVAR(p).","title":"Serial correlation and controls"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Forward-transformed-outcomes","page":"Guide","text":"Outcomes are often transformed with leads, e.g. a forward moving average ȳ_t = (y_t + y_{t+1} + y_{t+2} + y_{t+3})/4, built with lag and a negative shift. The trailing NaN padding is dropped automatically:\n\nma4(x) = (x .+ lag(x, -1) .+ lag(x, -2) .+ lag(x, -3)) ./ 4\nres_ma = spiv(ma4(y), ma4(Y), Z; H = 0:4:8, X = lags(hcat(y, Y, Z), 4))\ncoef(res_ma)\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7050592616489563\n\nxlags lags the series exactly as passed, so with forward-transformed y/Y it would put future values into the controls. Build the controls from the raw series with X = lags(...) instead, as above. Compare:\n\ncoef(spiv(ma4(y), ma4(Y), Z; H = 0:4:8, xlags = 4))   # biased: controls see the future\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.8693505732133051","title":"Forward-transformed outcomes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Weak-instrument-diagnostic","page":"Guide","text":"weak_iv_test returns a WeakIVDiagnostic flagging weak instruments, with bias tolerance tau (default 0.10) and size 1 - level. The design above is strongly identified:\n\nweak_iv_test(res_lp)\nWeakIVDiagnostic{Float64}(1192.5660555590143, 22.10444366576967, false)\n\nShrinking the instrument loading produces a weak design where is_weak flips to true:\n\nyw, Yw, Zw = make_dgp(; T = T, β_true = β_true, a = a, ρ = 0.8,\n    loading = 0.02, seed = 11)\nweak_iv_test(spiv(yw, Yw, Zw; H = H))\nWeakIVDiagnostic{Float64}(8.982876370850283, 21.874996871992618, true)","title":"Weak-instrument diagnostic"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Robust-confidence-sets","page":"Guide","text":"Under weak identification, invert the Anderson–Rubin (robust = :AR, default) or Kleibergen LM (robust = :KLM) statistic over a grid. robust_inference returns a RobustInference with per-parameter bounds; finite endpoints are refined by bisection between the last accepted and first rejected grid point.\n\nrobust_inference(spiv(y, Y, Z; H = H, robust = :AR)).bounds\n1×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.694987  0.711539\n\nrobust_inference(spiv(y, Y, Z; H = H, robust = :KLM)).bounds\n1×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.695456  0.711077\n\nAR or KLM. AR (χ² with H·Nz df) is fully robust and its sets are easy to read. KLM (χ² with K df) is more powerful when H·Nz ≫ K, but as a score test it can also accept points far from β̂, producing disconnected sets.\n\nSearch region. grid sets where the test is inverted: a number c means β̂ ± c·se (default 10), a tuple (lo, hi) applies to every parameter, and a K × 2 matrix gives per-parameter [lo hi] rows. grid_length sets the points per parameter (default 201 for K = 1, 101 for K = 2, 31 otherwise). Under weak instruments the strong-identification se is unreliable, so the default region can be too narrow. A bound of ±Inf means the accepted set reaches the edge of the region — it is unbounded, or extends beyond the grid, in that direction:\n\nres_w = spiv(yw, Yw, Zw; H = H, robust = :KLM)    # default grid: β̂ ± 10·se\nrobust_inference(res_w).bounds\n1×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.646373  Inf\n\nPassing an explicit range resolves the upper end:\n\nres_w = spiv(yw, Yw, Zw; H = H, robust = :KLM, grid = (-10, 10))\nrb = robust_inference(res_w)\n(bounds = rb.bounds, disconnected = rb.disconnected)\n(bounds = [0.6463725768702432 2.3503990143324995], disconnected = true)\n\nWhen disconnected is true the accepted points form more than one piece; bounds then report the hull (a conservative interval spanning the gaps), and the pieces can be read off rb.confidence_set. The printed summary flags both cases.","title":"Robust confidence sets"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/#Impulse-responses","page":"Guide","text":"The result carries the implied IRFs with Newey–West HAC standard errors as IRFBlocks, returned by irf: the outcome IRF (H × Nz, the default) and the endogenous-regressor IRF (H × K × Nz, response = :endogenous). Row i is horizon horizons(r)[i].\n\nUnits. Column n is the response to a one-standard-deviation shock to the n-th symmetrically whitened instrument (Z⊥Z⊥'/T)^{-1/2} z⊥_t (eq. 11); with a single instrument, a one-s.d. shock to z⊥. This is the scaling of the paper and of the Fieldhouse–Mertens code.\n\nStandard errors are a package choice — the paper and the Fieldhouse–Mertens code report point IRFs only. They are per-horizon Newey–West HAC sandwiches applied to a delta-method influence function that accounts for the estimated whitening matrix (treating it as known under-covers).\n\nirf(res_lp).point               # outcome IRF point estimates\n4×1 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.6882759844324327\n 0.429089191486194\n 0.1999936816095498\n 0.07428034265135139\n\nirf(res_lp).se                  # HAC standard errors\n4×1 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.012996781206407824\n 0.01620213662782255\n 0.01547414335636497\n 0.015083862458186442\n\nThe HAC bandwidth defaults to a fixed lag truncation equal to the horizon h (hac = :fixed; horizon 0 is White/HC0); pass hac = :neweywest or hac = :andrews for automatic-bandwidth selection. The Bartlett weights down-weight the overlap autocovariances, so when those are large (e.g. an outcome driven by several lags of the shock) the bands can under-cover somewhat at short horizons. With Plots loaded, plot(result) draws the outcome IRF bands against horizons(result) and plot(result; response = :endogenous) the endogenous IRFs (see examples/plots.jl).","title":"Impulse responses"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"Complete reference for the exported public API.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Estimator","page":"API","text":"","title":"Estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Specs","page":"API","text":"","title":"Specs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Result-types","page":"API","text":"","title":"Result types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Accessors","page":"API","text":"The result supports the StatsBase accessors coef, vcov, stderror, confint, coeftable, coefnames, nobs, dof_residual, and residuals, re-exported so that using SystemProjectionsIV alone is enough. confint and coeftable default to the level passed to spiv:\n\nSP-IV-specific accessors:","title":"Accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Data-helpers","page":"API","text":"Utilities for building lagged-control matrices for the X keyword of spiv. Both pad unavailable observations with NaN (leading for lags, trailing for leads, n < 0); spiv drops that padding from all inputs automatically. Integer input is promoted to floating point.","title":"Data helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.spiv","page":"API","text":"spiv(y, Y, Z, spec = SPIVwithLP(); H, X = nothing, intercept = true, xlags = 0,\n     robust = :AR, level = 0.95, tau = 0.10, grid = 10, grid_length = nothing,\n     hac = :fixed)\n\nSystem Projections IV estimator (Lewis & Mertens 2024).\n\nInputs use the time-in-rows convention (T periods down the rows); single series can be passed as plain vectors:\n\ny — outcome, length-T vector.\nY — endogenous regressors, T × K matrix (or a length-T vector when K = 1).\nZ — instruments, T × Nz matrix (or a length-T vector when Nz = 1).\n\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 8)                       # horizons 0,…,7; intercept-only controls\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 3:4:59)                  # a subset of horizons\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 8, xlags = 4)            # + 4 lags of [y Y z] as controls\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 8, X = lags(w, 4))       # + user-built lagged controls\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 8, robust = :KLM)        # KLM instead of AR robust set\nspiv(y, Y, z, SPIVwithVAR(4); H = 8)       # VAR(4) forecast errors\n\nspec selects the forecast-error construction:\n\nSPIVwithLP() (default) — Jordà local projections of y_{t+h}, Y_{t+h} on z_t and the controls (eq. A.1).\nSPIVwithVAR(p) — a VAR(p) on [Y_t, y_t, z_t]; forecast errors are built from the reduced-form residuals (eqs. A.2–A.3) and the controls (X, intercept, xlags) are ignored with a warning (the VAR's own lags play that role). T_eff = T − p − max(h).\n\nH sets the stacked horizons: an Int means h = 0,…,H-1; a vector (e.g. 3:4:59, as in Fieldhouse & Mertens) stacks exactly those horizons. Returns an SPIVResult{Float64, typeof(spec)} with the point estimate β̂ (eq. 9), the strong-identification sandwich variance (eqs. 18–19), the structural residuals, the weak-IV diagnostic (Proposition 7), and a robust confidence set.\n\nSample adjustment. Leading and trailing rows containing NaN in any input — the padding that lag / lags produce for lags and leads — are dropped from all inputs automatically (Stata/R-style), so X = lags(w, 4) or a forward moving average built with lag(y, -n) work as-is; the adjusted sample is visible as T_eff in the printed result. Any other non-finite value — Inf anywhere, or NaN inside the sample — is a data error and throws an ArgumentError.\n\nSerial correlation. The strong-identification standard errors use no HAR correction; this is valid when the controls absorb the serial correlation of z⊥ (docs/technical.md §5.4). The default controls are intercept-only, which suits a serially uncorrelated shock series as instrument; with a persistent instrument add lagged controls via xlags or X.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nX — additional controls to residualise on (e.g. lagged variables, X_{t-1}), a T × Nx matrix or a length-T vector; default none. Collinear columns are dropped with a warning.\nintercept — prepend a constant column to the controls (default true).\nxlags — also control for this many lags of [y Y Z] (in that column order), built and sample-adjusted automatically (default 0). The series are lagged exactly as passed: if y/Y are forward-transformed (e.g. a forward moving average), build the controls from the raw series with X = lags(...) instead. With xlags = p, the LP variant conditions on the same information set as SPIVwithVAR(p).\nrobust — weak-IV-robust test inverted for the confidence set: :AR (default; Anderson–Rubin, χ² with H·Nz df, fully robust) or :KLM (Kleibergen, χ² with K df; more powerful when H·Nz ≫ K).\nlevel — confidence level of the Wald intervals, the robust set, and the IRF bands (default 0.95). The weak-IV test is run at size 1 − level.\ntau — bias tolerance τ of the weak-IV test (Definition 3; default 0.10).\ngrid — search region for the robust set: a number c means β̂ ± c·se for each parameter (default 10); a tuple (lo, hi) applies to every parameter; a K × 2 matrix gives per-parameter [lo hi] rows. Use an explicit range when instruments are weak — the strong-identification se is then unreliable. Bounds that reach the edge of the region are reported as ±Inf.\ngrid_length — grid points per parameter (default 201 for K = 1, 101 for K = 2, 31 otherwise). Finite bounds are refined by bisection, so this only needs to be fine enough to locate the set.\nhac — bandwidth rule for the IRF HAC standard errors: :fixed (default; lag truncation = horizon, a package choice), :neweywest (automatic Newey–West 1994), or :andrews (automatic Andrews 1991).\n\nThe outcome IRF (H × Nz) and endogenous IRF (H × K × Nz), with per-horizon Newey–West HAC standard errors and level normal bands, are in result.irf_outcome / result.irf_endogenous (docs/technical.md §3.3, eq. 11), or via irf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.spiv"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.AbstractSPIVSpec","page":"API","text":"AbstractSPIVSpec\n\nAbstract supertype tagging which variant of the SP-IV estimator to run. The first step of spiv — constructing the residualised forecast errors — is dispatched on this type; the remaining estimation, inference, and IRF steps are shared. Concrete subtypes: SPIVwithLP and SPIVwithVAR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.AbstractSPIVSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVwithLP","page":"API","text":"SPIVwithLP() <: AbstractSPIVSpec\n\nLocal-projections variant of SP-IV. Forecast errors are built by projecting the leads y_{t+h} and Y_{t+h} on the instruments z_t and lagged controls X_{t-1} via direct local projections (technical.md §4.1, eq. A.1). This is the default spec for spiv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVwithLP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVwithVAR","page":"API","text":"SPIVwithVAR(p = 1) <: AbstractSPIVSpec\n\nVAR variant of SP-IV (forecast-error route). A VAR(p) is fit by OLS on the stacked vector [Y_t; y_t; z_t] and the h-step forecast errors are formed from its companion MA representation (technical.md §4.2, eqs. A.2–A.3). The VAR's own lags replace the LP control-residualisation, so the controls X passed to spiv are ignored.\n\nspiv(y, Y, z, SPIVwithVAR(4); H = 8)   # VAR(4) forecast errors\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVwithVAR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVResult","page":"API","text":"SPIVResult{T, S}\n\nThe bundle returned by spiv. All fields are concrete (no Union{T, Nothing}). It supports the StatsBase accessors coef, vcov, stderror, confint, coeftable, coefnames, nobs, dof_residual, and residuals.\n\nFields\n\nspec::S: the variant tag (SPIVwithLP or SPIVwithVAR).\nβ::Vector{T}: structural estimate β̂, length K (eq. 9).\nvcov::Matrix{T}: K × K sandwich covariance under strong identification (eqs. 18–19).\nresiduals::Matrix{T}: structural residuals u_H^⊥, shape H × T_eff.\nweak_iv::WeakIVDiagnostic{T}: first-stage weak-IV diagnostic.\nrobust::RobustInference{T}: AR/KLM robust confidence set.\nirf_outcome::IRFBlock{T, 2}: outcome IRF, H × Nz.\nirf_endogenous::IRFBlock{T, 3}: endogenous IRF, H × K × Nz.\nhorizons::Vector{Int}: the stacked horizons (0:H-1 by default).\nlevel::T: confidence level shared by confint, the robust set, and the IRF bands.\nH, K, Nz, Nx, T_eff::Int: number of horizons, endogenous regressors, instruments, controls (the rank of the control matrix), and effective sample size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.SPIVResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.WeakIVDiagnostic","page":"API","text":"WeakIVDiagnostic{T}\n\nOutcome of the first-stage weak-instrument test (technical.md §6, Proposition 7 and Theorem 1). Retrieve it from a result with weak_iv_test.\n\nFields\n\ng_min::T: the minimum g statistic; reduces to the first-stage F in the just-identified scalar case.\ncritical_value::T: the (moment-matched, conservative) critical value it is compared against.\nis_weak::Bool: true when g_min falls below critical_value, flagging weak instruments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.WeakIVDiagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.RobustInference","page":"API","text":"RobustInference{T}\n\nWeak-identification-robust confidence set, obtained by inverting the Anderson–Rubin (eq. 20) or Kleibergen KLM (eq. 21) statistic over a grid (technical.md §7). Retrieve it from a result with robust_inference.\n\nFields\n\nmethod::Symbol: :AR, :KLM, or :none (the unpopulated stub).\ncritical_value::T: the chi-squared critical value used to accept grid points.\nbounds::Matrix{T}: K × 2 per-parameter (lower, upper): the hull of the accepted set, with each finite endpoint refined by bisection between the last accepted and first rejected grid point. A bound of -Inf/Inf means the set reaches the edge of the search grid — it is unbounded (or extends beyond the grid) in that direction; widen grid in spiv to explore further. NaN when no grid point is accepted (the set is empty, or narrower than the grid step). For K ≥ 2 the refinement runs along grid lines, so bounds are accurate to the grid resolution.\nconfidence_set::Matrix{T}: the accepted grid points, N_accepted × K (rows = points, columns = parameters).\ndegrees_freedom::Int: degrees of freedom of the reference distribution.\ndisconnected::Bool: true when the accepted grid points form more than one connected piece; bounds then span the gaps (a conservative hull) and the pieces can be read off confidence_set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.RobustInference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.IRFBlock","page":"API","text":"IRFBlock{T, N}\n\nA block of impulse responses with HAC standard errors and confidence bands (technical.md §3.3). Parameterised on dimensionality N:\n\nN = 2 for the outcome IRF, shape H × Nz;\nN = 3 for the endogenous IRF, shape H × K × Nz.\n\nRow i is horizon horizons(r)[i]. Column n is the response to a one-standard- deviation shock to the n-th symmetrically whitened instrument (Z⊥Z⊥'/T)^{-1/2} z⊥_t (eq. 11); with a single instrument, a one-s.d. shock to z⊥.\n\nFields\n\npoint::Array{T, N}: point estimates.\nse::Array{T, N}: Newey–West HAC standard errors (delta-method, accounting for the estimated whitening matrix).\nlower::Array{T, N}, upper::Array{T, N}: confidence-band endpoints.\n\nAll four arrays share the same shape. Access these on an SPIVResult via result.irf_outcome and result.irf_endogenous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.IRFBlock"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.confint-Tuple{SPIVResult}","page":"API","text":"confint(r::SPIVResult; level = r.level) -> Matrix\n\nK × 2 strong-identification (Wald) confidence intervals β̂ ± z·se, using normal critical values (the asymptotic theory of docs/technical.md §5.3). The default level is the one passed to spiv. The Fieldhouse–Mertens MATLAB code uses Student-t quantiles with dof_residual(r) degrees of freedom instead; the difference vanishes with T_eff. Under weak instruments use robust_inference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.confint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#StatsAPI.coeftable-Tuple{SPIVResult}","page":"API","text":"coeftable(r::SPIVResult; level = r.level) -> StatsBase.CoefTable\n\nCoefficient table (estimate, std. error, z, two-sided normal p-value, Wald confidence interval) under strong identification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.coeftable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.horizons","page":"API","text":"horizons(r::SPIVResult) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe horizons h stacked in the estimation (0:H-1 unless a vector was passed as H to spiv).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.horizons"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.weak_iv_test","page":"API","text":"weak_iv_test(r::SPIVResult) -> WeakIVDiagnostic\n\nThe first-stage weak-instrument diagnostic. See WeakIVDiagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.weak_iv_test"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.robust_inference","page":"API","text":"robust_inference(r::SPIVResult) -> RobustInference\n\nThe weak-identification-robust (AR/KLM) confidence set. See RobustInference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.robust_inference"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.spec","page":"API","text":"spec(r::SPIVResult) -> AbstractSPIVSpec\n\nThe variant tag the result was produced with (SPIVwithLP or SPIVwithVAR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.irf","page":"API","text":"irf(r::SPIVResult; response = :outcome) -> IRFBlock\n\nThe impulse responses to the instrumented shocks, with HAC standard errors and normal bands: response = :outcome (default) returns the outcome IRF (H × Nz), response = :endogenous the endogenous-regressor IRF (H × K × Nz). See IRFBlock for the point/se/lower/upper fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.irf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.summary-Tuple{IO, SPIVResult}","page":"API","text":"summary([io::IO,] r::SPIVResult)\n\nPrint the full estimation summary — coefficient table, weak-IV verdict, robust confidence set, and IRF shapes — like R's summary(). Identical to the REPL display of r; returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.lag","page":"API","text":"lag(x::AbstractVector, n::Int; default=NaN)\n\nCreate lagged version of vector x by n periods.\n\nArguments\n\nx::AbstractVector: Input vector\nn::Int: Number of lags (positive for lags, negative for leads)\ndefault: Value to fill for unavailable observations (default: NaN)\n\nExamples\n\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nlag(x, 1)   # [NaN, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\nlag(x, -2)  # [3.0, 4.0, 5.0, NaN, NaN]  (lead)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.lag"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SystemProjectionsIV.lags","page":"API","text":"lags(x::AbstractVecOrMat, p::Int; default=NaN)\n\nMatrix of the first p lags of x: columns [lag(x,1) … lag(x,p)], T × (n_vars·p) (lag-major column order for matrix input). The first p rows are padded with default (NaN); spiv drops that burn-in from all inputs automatically, so the result can be passed as controls directly:\n\nspiv(y, Y, z; H = 8, X = lags(x, p))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.lags"},{"category":"section","location":"#SystemProjectionsIV.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia implementation of the System Projections IV (SP-IV) estimator of Lewis & Mertens (2024). SP-IV identifies structural macroeconomic relationships (such as the Phillips Curve) by regressing impulse responses to external instruments on each other, estimated with local projections or VARs. Because identification comes from the responses at multiple horizons, a rich set of lagged controls can be included — so only contemporaneous and lead exogeneity of the instruments is required — without shrinking the instruments' explanatory power to that of the contemporaneous shock, as happens in IV with distributed lags of shocks as instruments.\n\nnote: Note\nThis is an independent, unofficial implementation of the SP-IV methodology developed by Lewis & Mertens (2024). It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the paper's authors, and any implementation errors are the package author's own.\n\nThe structural equation\n\ny_t = beta Y_t + u_t\n\nis estimated by regressing the impulse responses of y_t to the external shocks Z on the impulse responses of Y_t to the same shocks, after residualising on lagged controls X_t-1. Relative to 2SLS with distributed lags of shocks as instruments, this imposes weaker exogeneity requirements and can improve efficiency and increase effective instrument strength; instruments can still be weak, hence the weak-IV test and robust confidence sets.","title":"SystemProjectionsIV.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The package is not registered. Install directly from the repository:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/andrerecio/SystemProjectionsIV.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using SystemProjectionsIV, Random\n\n# Simulate a small SP-IV design: y_t = β·Y_t + u_t, where Y responds to an\n# external instrument ε through a known impulse response and is endogenous (Y\n# correlates with the structural error u).\nRandom.seed!(1234)\nT, H, β = 400, 8, 0.5\nε = randn(T)                          # external instrument / shock\nu = 0.3 .* randn(T)\nY = zeros(T)\nfor t in 1:T, j in 0:3\n    t - j ≥ 1 && (Y[t] += 0.8^j * ε[t - j])\nend\nY .+= 0.7 .* u .+ 0.2 .* randn(T)     # endogeneity\n\ny = β .* Y .+ u\n\n# y outcome, Y endogenous regressor(s), ε instrument(s); vectors or matrices\n# both work, and an intercept is included by default.\nresult = spiv(y, Y, ε; H = H, robust = :AR)\nSystem Projections IV — local projections (LP)\n  horizons h=0:7 (H=8), K=1 endogenous, Nz=1 instruments, Nx=1 controls, T_eff=393\n\nCoefficients (strong-identification sandwich):\n  param      estimate   std.err.         z   95% CI\n  β[1]         0.5051     0.0092     54.80   [0.4870, 0.5231]\n\nWeak-IV (g): 53.063   critical value: 21.927   ⇒ instruments strong\nRobust (AR, 95%, df=8): β[1]∈[0.4827, 0.5236]\nIRFs: outcome (8, 1), endogenous (8, 1, 1) (point/se/lower/upper in result.irf_*)\n\nThe returned SPIVResult carries the structural estimate, its sandwich covariance under strong identification, residuals, the weak-IV diagnostic, the robust (AR or KLM) confidence set, and the implied IRFs with HAC standard errors. The StatsBase accessors are re-exported, so no extra using is needed:\n\ncoef(result)            # β̂\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.5050697183294974\n\nconfint(result)         # strong-identification CI at `level` (default 0.95)\n1×2 Matrix{Float64}:\n 0.487005  0.523135\n\ncoeftable(result)\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n        Coef.  Std. Error      z  Pr(>|z|)  Lower 95%  Upper 95%\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nβ[1]  0.50507  0.00921691  54.80    <1e-99   0.487005   0.523135\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nweak_iv_test(result)    # g statistic, critical value, is_weak\nWeakIVDiagnostic{Float64}(53.06263466617511, 21.926517339455913, false)\n\nirf(result).point       # outcome IRF point estimates (H × Nz)\n8×1 Matrix{Float64}:\n  0.5011951877740699\n  0.43894282338961244\n  0.35714446705118086\n  0.269938362302246\n  0.033888974839775675\n -0.0220633309614191\n  0.03335347408720139\n -0.007005870264930401\n\nsummary(result) prints the full estimation table, and lagged controls are one keyword away — spiv(y, Y, ε; H = H, xlags = 4) conditions on four lags of all the variables, with the NaN padding of lags and leads dropped automatically. H = 8 stacks horizons 0:7; pass a vector such as H = 3:4:59 to stack a subset.\n\nSee the Guide for choosing horizons, the LP and VAR variants, controls, weak-instrument-robust inference, and IRF options, and the API for the full reference. The complete mathematical specification lives in docs/technical.md in the repository.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#References","page":"Home","text":"Lewis, D. J., & Mertens, K. (2024). Dynamic Identification Using System Projections on Instrumental Variables. Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas Working Paper No. 2204 (revised July 2024). doi:10.24149/wp2204r3","title":"References"}]
}
